If you want to use a radial grid in the X(horizontal) plane relative to the rotation of the planets around the Sun, you can modify the drawGrid function to calculate the position of each grid line based on its distance from the Sun’s position along the X(horizontal) axis. Here’s an example of how you could modify the function to achieve this:


function drawGrid() {
  const gridSpacing = 100;

  // Find the minimum and maximum X(horizontal) positions of the planets
  const minPosX = Math.min(sunPos.x, mercuryPos.x, venusPos.x, earthPos.x);
  const maxPosX = Math.max(sunPos.x, mercuryPos.x, venusPos.x, earthPos.x);

  // Draw grid lines that lie on the X(horizontal) axis
  ctx.beginPath();
  ctx.strokeStyle = “rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.2)”;
  for (let i = Math.floor(minPosX / gridSpacing) * gridSpacing; i <= maxPosX; i += gridSpacing) {
    const p1 = { x: i, y: -gridSize, z: 0 };
    const p2 = { x: i, y: gridSize, z: 0 };
    ctx.moveTo(projectTo2D(p1.x, p1.y, p1.z).x, projectTo2D(p1.x, p1.y, p1.z).y);
    ctx.lineTo(projectTo2D(p2.x, p2.y, p2.z).x, projectTo2D(p2.x, p2.y, p2.z).y);
  }
  ctx.stroke();
  ctx.closePath();
}



In this modified version, the minPosX and maxPosX variables are calculated as the minimum and maximum X(horizontal) positions of the planets. The for loop then iterates over the X(horizontal) positions within the range of minPosX and maxPosX, with a step size of gridSpacing. For each position, p1 and p2 are calculated with the X(horizontal) position as the x coordinate and gridSize as the
